{
  "dom_id": "step-q127296",
  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "Find the Cartesian equation of the osculating plane for a curve $r(t)$ at the point $P(5,2,1)$ where $t=3,$ given that\n\n$$\nT(3)=\\frac{1}{5}(3\\,i+4\\,j),\\,N(3)=\\frac{1}{5}(4\\,i-3\\,j).\n$$\n\nNote that $T$ and $N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively.",
  "worked_solution": "First, notice that the position vector of the point $P(5,2,1)$ is $p=⟨5,2,1⟩.$\n\nTo find the equation of the osculating plane, we need to find a vector that's perpendicular to it. To do that, we find the cross product of the unit tangent $T(3)$ and the principal normal $N(3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(3)\\times N(3) & =\\frac{1}{5}(3\\,i+4\\,j)\\times \\frac{1}{5}(4\\,i-3\\,j) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25}\\cdot \\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 3 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25}[\\text{ }\\left|\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,i-\\left|\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,j+\\left|\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25}(-25\\,k) \\\\  & =-k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our perpendicular vector $n$ is \n\n$$\nn=T(3)\\times N(3)=-k=⟨0,0,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nFinally, let $⟨x,y,z⟩$ denote the position vector of a general point on the osculating plane. Since the plane is perpendicular to $n$ and passes through the tip of the position vector $p$, its Cartesian equation can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(⟨x,y,z⟩-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ ⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot n-p\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ ⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ ⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨0,0,-1⟩ & =⟨5,2,1⟩\\cdot ⟨0,0,-1⟩ \\\\ x(0)+y(0)+z(-1) & =5(0)+2(0)+1(-1) \\\\ 0+0-z & =0+0-1 \\\\ z-1 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
